<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anna Komnene</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1083–<a href="/years/1153">1153</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Anna Comnena · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komnenian-dynasty">Komnenian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anna Komnene was a Byzantine Greek princess and historian, author of the Alexiad, an account of the reign of her father, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a>, and the most important primary source for Byzantine history of the late 11th and early 12th centuries and for the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a>. Born in 1083 in the Porphyra Chamber of the palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, she was betrothed to Constantine Doukas, with whom she was next in line to the throne until her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-komnenos">John II Komnenos</a> became heir in 1092. After 1118 she is commonly believed to have plotted against John in favour of her husband, Nikephoros Bryennios, though no contemporary evidence shows it; she ended her life in the Kecharitomene convent.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Anna was born on 1 December 1083 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a> and Irene Doukaina, the eldest of seven children. Alexios had become emperor in 1081 by usurping Nikephoros Botaneiates. She was born in the Porphyra Chamber of the imperial palace, which made her a porphyrogenita. At birth she was betrothed to Constantine Doukas, son of Emperor Michael VII, probably as a political match to support the legitimacy of her father, and from about 1090 she was raised in the household of his mother, Maria of Alania.</p><p>Her education covered Greek literature, philosophy, theology, mathematics and medicine. Georgios Tornikes said in a funeral oration that she read the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> in secret from her parents. She gained expertise in medicine, helped treat her father in his last illness and administered a large hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Anna and Constantine were heirs until her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-komnenos">John II Komnenos</a> was designated emperor in 1092. Constantine died around 1094, and about 1097 she married Nikephoros Bryennios, a soldier and historian whose family had held the throne before her father. When Alexios died in 1118, John was acclaimed emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niketas-choniates">Niketas Choniates</a>, writing after 1204, says Anna was driven by ambition and revenge to plot against John, that Bryennios refused to join her, and that she forfeited her estates. Leonora Neville argues she was probably not involved, since only Choniates portrays her as a rebel. After her husband's death she entered the Kecharitomene convent, founded by her mother, and stayed there for the rest of her life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In the convent Anna studied philosophy and history and held scholarly gatherings, including those on Aristotelian studies. She wrote the Alexiad in the mid-1140s or the 1150s, citing her husband's unfinished history as a reason. She drew on old soldiers, accounts from her father and uncles, and the imperial archives. It is written in Attic Greek modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>.</p><p>The Alexiad describes weapons, tactics and battles and relations between Alexios and the West. She wrote of events of her childhood, and wrote to praise her father and denigrate his successors. Still, it is the only Byzantine eyewitness account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>, giving the alarm felt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> at the western armies, whom she calls <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anna Komnene” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Anna Komnene</b> said the Pechenegs and Cumans shared a common language.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pechenegs">Pechenegs · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Imperial women could hold real power: four emperors owed their throne to their link with the empress Zoe, and her sister Theodora ruled alone in 1055–1056, while <b>Anna Komnene</b> wrote the history of her father Alexios I’s reign.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1128" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anna Komnene"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1128">What was here in 1128? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
